Before every committee meeting you take the same handful of
drafts and decide which ones are ready for the packet. The
files may have spaces in their names; one person might forget
to supply an attachment. You don't need a program to run the
meeting. A small shell script can do the repetitive part:
check the paths and report what would be included. It will
not move, copy, open or publish the documents.

\begin{objectives}
\item Move a proven command sequence into a plain-text script with an explicit interpreter.
\item Use a test, a branch and a loop to check a short list of arguments.
\item Run a preview on safe fixtures and interpret its separate output streams and status.
\end{objectives}

\section{Start with a single decision}
\label{sec:30-if}
\index{shell scripts}
\index{conditionals}
\index{test@\texttt{test}}

The first useful thing to decide is whether one candidate is
a regular file. Start a fresh \texttt{sh} in the directory
containing the book's downloaded source; later you will run
the supplied script without copying it. Type each of these
lines separately. The first saves the absolute path of that
script, using \texttt{pwd -P} to obtain the physical path of
the current directory. \texttt{-P} resolves symbolic links
in the working-directory path. Stop unless the displayed
status is \texttt{0}; see Chapter~\ref{ch:11} if making a plain
text file yourself instead. The \texttt{-f} test checks for
a regular file and the \cmd|$?| expansion reads the latest
exit status before another command replaces it.

\begin{terminal}
script="$(pwd -P)/listings/chapter-30/preview-notes.sh"
test -f "$script"
printf 'Script found? status %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Once you know the script path, set up the fixture. Stop if
\texttt{mktemp -d} reports an error or if the \texttt{cd}
line does not show the new directory. This convenient form
is common on GNU and BSD systems; if unavailable, use a new
empty practice directory from Chapter~\ref{ch:12} and save
its absolute path as \texttt{practice}. The output redirections
below are safe in that newly created directory, not in a
directory where the names might already hold real notes.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$practice" && pwd
printf '%s\n' 'Sample minutes' > 'May notes.txt'
printf '%s\n' 'Sample agenda' > agenda.txt
mkdir 'Handouts'
\end{terminal}

The three fixture entries represent a name with a space, a
simple name and a directory. A fourth candidate, \texttt{absent.txt},
will be deliberately missing. This spread matters. If you
test only a simple file that exists, you learn neither whether
quotes survived nor whether the script distinguishes a
directory from a regular file.

\begin{terminal}
test -f './May notes.txt'
printf 'Present: %s\n' "$?"
test -f './Handouts'
printf 'Directory: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Present: 0} and \texttt{Directory: 1}. The
ordinary shell convention is that zero signals success or a
true condition. False is often one; a malformed invocation
may have a different nonzero status. The test prints nothing
when it can answer normally. You have read status both times
rather than trying to interpret an empty screen.

\begin{terminal}
if [ -f './May notes.txt' ]; then
    printf '%s\n' 'Found a regular file'
else
    printf '%s\n' 'No regular file there'
fi
\end{terminal}

This is the first piece worth keeping. \texttt{if} runs
the command before \texttt{then} and branches according
to its status. The opening bracket is another spelling of
the \texttt{test} command; the closing bracket is its final
argument, so leave spaces around both brackets. The
semicolon ends the test command on the same line as
\texttt{then}. \texttt{else} gives the other branch and
\texttt{fi} ends the whole structure. Expect \texttt{Found
a regular file}. Replace the fixture filename with
\texttt{./Handouts} to see the other branch.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-30/test.tikz}
\caption{The test's status selects the branch.}
\label{fig:30-test}
\VisualDescription{A regular-file test branches at status zero to report a found file, and at nonzero status to report no regular file.}
\end{figure}

\section{Repeat the decision on existing arguments}
\label{sec:30-loop}
\index{loops}
\index{positional parameters}

There is a short way to apply one decision to a group of
filenames, without writing a separate command for each. Try
a loop that merely displays its words. First, in your practice
shell, use \texttt{set --} to create a list of arguments as
in Chapter~\ref{ch:27}. Then let \texttt{for} assign each
argument in turn to the variable \texttt{note}. The action
between \texttt{do} and \texttt{done} runs once per argument.

\begin{terminal}
set -- './May notes.txt' './agenda.txt' './absent.txt'
for note in "$@"; do
    printf 'Candidate: <%s>\n' "$note"
done
\end{terminal}

Expect one candidate line for each path, including the
missing one. No file test happens here; the loop works with
strings. The quotation marks around \cmd|"$@"| preserve the
boundary around each existing argument, and the marks around
\cmd|"$note"| preserve that boundary during the current
iteration. Breaking either boundary can make a valid
space-containing name look like several missing files.
To turn the display into a check, place the test from
Figure~\ref{fig:30-test} inside the loop and use
\cmd|"$note"| in place of the fixed pathname.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-30/loop.tikz}
\caption{One body runs three times, once per argument.}
\label{fig:30-loop}
\VisualDescription{Three candidates, May notes, agenda and absent, enter the same check individually. The space in May notes stays inside one candidate.}
\end{figure}

\section{Read the complete preview before running it}
\label{sec:30-listing}
\index{dry run}

The listing adds a few rules around the loop. No arguments
means a usage error. The \texttt{-eq} test compares numbers:
here it asks whether the argument count \cmd|$#| is zero.
\cmd|>&2| directs the message to standard error, leaving
standard output free for successful candidates. The script
uses status two for misuse. For each supplied name, the
\texttt{case} checks its spelling against shell patterns:
\cmd|./*| accepts a relative path prefixed with dot-slash
and \cmd|/*| accepts an absolute path. The vertical bar
separates acceptable alternatives, and \cmd|;;| ends a
branch. Those pattern stars match text in the word;
unlike a standalone shell argument they do not enumerate
directory entries. \texttt{continue} skips to the next
iteration after an unacceptable spelling.

\ShellFile{listings/chapter-30/preview-notes.sh}

In the file check, \texttt{-f} asks for a regular file and
\texttt{-r} asks whether the file is readable; \cmd|&&|
requires the first test to succeed before running the second.
The script itself does not read file contents. A symbolic link
to a readable regular file may pass; this is not a check for
links or a promise that any path points within a certain
directory. Likewise, a result can become stale if the file
changes later. The script is intentionally only a preview,
not an authorization step or a production publishing tool.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.3in}YY}
\toprule
Input case & Effect for this invocation & Where to look \\
\midrule
No path arguments & Show usage and stop, status 2. & Standard error \\
Every candidate passes & Report each candidate and return status 0. & Standard output \\
Some candidate fails & Report the failure, keep checking and return status 1. & Standard error for the failure; standard output for passing items \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

The \texttt{result} variable remembers failure across
iterations. A successful item after a rejected one does not
set it back to zero. At the end \texttt{exit} returns the
remembered result to whoever ran the script. It exits
the child shell, not the caller's interactive session,
because we run a separate \texttt{sh}, rather than reading
the file as source in the current shell.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-30/result.tikz}
\caption{One rejection determines the final status even if other checks pass.}
\label{fig:30-result}
\VisualDescription{The script starts with result zero. A rejected item changes it to one. A later passing item leaves it at one for the final exit.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Why stop at a preview?}
\Brian{The script already knows which files pass. Why not add a copy command now?}
\Stephen{Because passing means only that a pathname passed two checks. We haven't decided where to copy or what to do if the destination already has that name.}
\Brian{But I can see where the names would go next. Isn't that enough?}
\Stephen{To decide what to include, yes. To change files, we also need a destination rule, an overwrite policy and a way to verify the result.}
\Brian{So I can use this script regularly even if it never copies anything?}
\Stephen{Exactly. A quick check before handling files by hand is already a useful repeatable task.}
\DialogueConclusion{A useful preview need not change files.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Run the fixture and check all three outcomes}
\label{sec:30-run}

First run the syntax check, then display its exit status.
\texttt{sh -n} reads commands without executing them. It
can catch an omitted \texttt{fi} or an unclosed quote, but
it cannot tell whether the test checks the right file.

\begin{terminal}
sh -n "$script"
printf 'Syntax status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Syntax status: 0}. If it is nonzero, stop and
examine the first error before running the file. Next, invoke
the file with a good path and read the status:

\begin{terminal}
sh "$script" './May notes.txt'
printf 'Preview status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Would include: <./May notes.txt>} and status
zero. \texttt{sh} opens the script from the stored absolute
path, while the candidate \texttt{./May notes.txt} is resolved
relative to the working directory printed during setup. The
directory holding the script and the directory holding the
candidate are independent; starting a script does not
automatically change the working directory.

\begin{terminal}
sh "$script" './absent.txt' './agenda.txt'
printf 'Preview status: %s\n' "$?"
sh "$script"
printf 'Usage status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

Expect a rejection for the absent name, an inclusion for the
agenda and \texttt{Preview status: 1}. With no arguments, a
usage message and \texttt{Usage status: 2} should follow.
If you use different stream redirections or a different
display, do not infer status from the visible order of
standard output and standard error.

\begin{quicktable}{YY}
\toprule
Observation & Next useful check \\
\midrule
All files rejected after moving to another folder & Use \texttt{pwd}; relative candidates follow the current directory. \\
An argument with spaces became two failures & Check the caller's quotes first, then inspect \texttt{"\$@"} inside the loop. \\
No candidates but a usage message & Check that you supplied arguments after the script path. \\
Syntactically valid script, wrong inclusion & Add a fixture that distinguishes the intended condition from the one written. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

If you save the listing yourself, save plain text, not a
word-processing document. Run it explicitly as shown; it
does not need an executable permission change for
\texttt{sh} to read it. The first line records its intended
interpreter for other invocation methods discussed in
Chapter~\ref{ch:29}. Use these checks on invented inputs
before adopting an edited version. Do not feed the resulting
report into a shell: it is for your eyes, and a filename can
contain angle brackets, newlines and other misleading
characters. Keep real paths as individually quoted arguments.

\section{Write down a contract before adding a feature}
\label{sec:30-contract}

You can now explain the script in four questions. What
does it accept? A list of individually supplied paths.
What does it reject? The specified bad spellings, missing
files, directories and paths that fail the readability
test. How does it respond? A passing path goes to standard
output; rejections go to standard error with a nonzero
status, even if another item passed. What does it change?
Nothing on disk during an ordinary invocation. Those
answers form a small contract the reader can verify.

An attractive extension would be to include every text
file without typing their names. Try the existing
script with an intentional glob in the calling shell,
not with a string containing an apparent list:

\begin{terminal}
printf 'Candidate: <%s>\n' ./*.txt
sh "$script" ./*.txt
printf 'Preview status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The fixture should yield two candidates and status zero.
The caller has expanded the glob, so the script receives
two actual path strings. The space in \texttt{May notes.txt}
does not split a later time because the loop preserves its
arguments. If there were no matches, a POSIX \texttt{sh}
would pass the original pattern and our file test would
reject the nonexistent path. With a shell that stops on
an unmatched glob, the script would not run at all. If
you experiment after creating \texttt{accepted.txt} or
\texttt{rejected.txt} in an exercise, remove those fixture
reports first: otherwise they also match the pattern.

Adding a feature such as actually copying files would change
the last answer in the contract. Before doing that, the
destination must be explicit and non-overwriting behavior
must be decided and proven on disposable files. A file
manager could be the right tool for that last step if its
preview and confirmations already do what you want.
Do not weaken the tests just to automate the entire task.
It may be enough to run the preview, resolve the missing
items, and then assemble the packet by hand.

Keep a known-good invocation alongside the saved script.
For this example it names one file containing spaces and
one missing file first, followed by a passing one. That
checks argument handling and proves that processing continues
after a rejection without forgetting the final status.
When the script grows, change one behavior and rerun the
previous checks before adding another. A live folder with
dozens of files is a poor way to tell whether a change works:
when the result differs you don't know if the script,
the inputs or the working directory changed.

\section{Debug the caller before rewriting the loop}
\label{sec:30-debug}

The committee's next run returns two errors for a
document you know exists. Before making the script
more complicated, replace it at your practice prompt
with the old argument probe. Compare the two versions
below. They print rather than open anything.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' ./May notes.txt './agenda.txt'
printf '<%s>\n' './May notes.txt' './agenda.txt'
\end{terminal}

The first shows \texttt{<./May>} and \texttt{<notes.txt>}
before the agenda; the second shows the intended two
path arguments. The script never had the missing
space to recover in the first version. It might even
pass one of those unintended names if another file
of that name happened to exist. You cannot fix a
misdelivered list by adding quotes inside the receiver
after the split. Correct the invocation and keep using
\cmd|"$@"| inside the script.

A different failure is ``right arguments, wrong
directory.'' Check the absolute script location with
the argument probe and the input location with
\texttt{pwd}. The former tells you which source file
the interpreter should read; the latter tells you the
base for a relative input path. They can differ and
that need not be a problem. What matters is which one
the caller expected to use. When sending a procedure
to someone else, include both pieces explicitly:
which interpreter and source file to open, and from
which folder to run the invocation.

Another check is whether the script prints a rejection
but returns success. Run the failure first and again
last when checking edits. A script that blindly uses
the last test status may look fine with the failure
last and get the other ordering wrong. The stored
\texttt{result} avoids that mistake. Your record of
the run should include the status and both streams,
not just a screenshot of the last line. If the
correct output arrives with a wrong status, the
script is still misleading to anything making a
decision from its result.

For an everyday task, stop once this procedure is
small enough to understand and easy enough to repeat.
Keep a copy of the working script alongside the
documentation that says how to invoke it. Don't change
your login shell, edit workplace policy or schedule
the job just to see whether it works. A repeatable
manual invocation is already automation: it moves
the tedious checks out of your memory while leaving
the final decision in your hands.

\section{Exercises and cleanup}

\begin{exercise}{Include two real fixtures}
\label{ex:30-passes}
Invoke the script once with both regular fixture files.
Check whether it knows the names separately and what status
it gives. This should not create a report file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sh "$script" './May notes.txt' './agenda.txt'
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
Expect two inclusion lines and zero. The fixtures stay
unchanged; remove them with the general cleanup below.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Separate failures and successes}
\label{ex:30-streams}
Run the preview with \texttt{./Handouts} and
\texttt{./agenda.txt}. In the practice directory only,
save the two streams in distinct new files, then read them.
Confirm the status before reading either file. The
\texttt{2>} operator opens a file for standard error.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sh "$script" './Handouts' './agenda.txt' > accepted.txt 2> rejected.txt
printf '%s\n' "$?"
cat accepted.txt
cat rejected.txt
rm -i './accepted.txt' './rejected.txt'
\end{terminal}
Expect one, then the inclusion line, then the rejection
line. Confirm that you are still in the fixture directory
before approving the two explicit removals.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Challenge a command-like spelling}
\label{ex:30-operand}
Pass a candidate called \texttt{-n} and then a valid
fixture path to the script, without creating any new file.
Check that the first argument does not become a \texttt{sh}
option or corrupt the second argument.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sh "$script" '-n' './May notes.txt'
printf '%s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
The first candidate gets the dot-slash or absolute path
diagnostic. The second still passes. Expect status one;
there is nothing extra to clean up. The arguments after
the script path belong to that script.
\end{solution}

Verify the practice directory with \texttt{pwd} before
removing the two named files. The empty \texttt{Handouts}
directory is removable with \texttt{rmdir}; after
leaving your practice folder, use the same command on the
folder itself. If it is not empty, inspect the contents rather
than forcing removal. If you made your own copy of the script,
keep it wherever you chose to save it or remove just that copy.

\begin{terminal}
pwd
rm -i './May notes.txt' './agenda.txt'
rmdir './Handouts'
cd / && rmdir "$practice"
exit
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Build a modest script from one tested decision, then repeat it on individually quoted arguments.
\item Use separate output streams and a documented status so that both people and later commands can interpret the result.
\item A syntax check is not a behavior test; try known passes and failures in a disposable workspace.
\end{takeaways}

